> The half with the most people is not the same half. It holds 92.7% of the world's people, and on the population maps I used its best centre has moved: it is in the western Alps in every year I tried up to 2010, and near Berlin from 2020 on (the two later years I tried, 2025 and 2030, are projections). The difference is a sliver on each side of the world. The half centred near Berlin reaches Manila, Kuala Lumpur and Singapore; the half centred in the Alps reaches São Paulo, the south of Brazil and most of Ecuador instead. In 1975 the South American sliver held more people, 41 million against 30 million. By 2020 the Asian one did, 92 million against 87 million. The lead in 2020 is thin: between 2.6 and 4.9 million people, depending on the map, out of nearly 8,000 million.
> 
> 
> A map of that half had been published already: Bill Rankin's "Human Hemisphere" of 2015, for the people of the year 2000, with its centre in western Switzerland. The two do not disagree, because they are for different years. A search of the web found nobody who had said that the centre has moved, which is not proof that nobody has.
> 
> 
> This study began with a few random words that had been sent to me, and one of them was woodpeckers. Going by where woodpeckers have been recorded, and counting a kind found on both sides of the cut in part, the half with the most kinds of woodpecker holds about 83% of them. It is centred in Gascony, in the south-west of France, give or take 300 km.
