< The same question can be asked about people. The half with the most people holds 92.7% of them, and when I computed it for 2020 its centre came out a little west of Berlin. A published map, Bill Rankin's of 2015, puts the centre in western Switzerland, with 92.9% of the people of the year 2000. The two do not disagree, because they are for different years. The population maps I used show the world's people for a series of years, and in every year I tried up to 2010 the best centre is in the western Alps. From 2020 on it is near Berlin (the two later years I tried, 2025 and 2030, are projections), and in 2015 it is too close to call between there and a place in Bavaria. Two other series of population maps, made by another group, agree as far as they go. The older series has the centre in the Alps in 2000 and 2010 and near Berlin in 2020. The newer series begins in 2015 and has the centre near Berlin from then on; unlike the maps I first used, it knows the censuses taken around 2020. On all three the lead is thin: in 2020 the half centred near Berlin holds between 2.6 and 4.9 million more people than the half centred in the Alps, out of nearly 8,000 million.
